<Base title="WAL, checkpoints, and recovery" description="Follow actual WAL and page states through a crash, derive redo from page progress, and distinguish durable commit, full-page images, checkpoints and recovery history.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <StorageDefs />
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Inside PostgreSQL</p><h1>WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
 <p>After PostgreSQL reports that a transaction committed, some of its changed table pages can still exist only in memory. Losing that memory must not lose the committed result. Write-ahead logging makes this possible by preserving another description of the changes before success is acknowledged. Recovery can use that description to bring older stored pages forward.</p>
 <p>This does not make every logged change a successful transaction. PostgreSQL also logs work that later rolls back, and it can write a page containing an uncommitted version. Recovering the physical page and deciding whether a version is visible are separate jobs. We will follow both through a stock update, then explain how checkpoints and page positions keep recovery from applying the wrong work.</p>
 <p>The server experiment used PostgreSQL 18.6 in a disposable local cluster with logged tables, <code>fsync=on</code>, <code>full_page_writes=on</code>, <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> and no synchronous standby. It tested an immediate server shutdown and restart, not a power failure or a storage device’s honesty about flushes. The browser lesson demonstrates WAL generation and rollback; its embedded storage has a different durability environment.</p>
 <section id="records"><h2>A logged change is not a commit</h2>
 <p>Updating stock from three to one changes heap-version data and metadata in a shared buffer. Indexes and other persistent structures may also need changes. PostgreSQL emits WAL records that describe the recoverable work. These records are organised around database storage operations; they are not a saved list of the original SQL statements or an application audit log.</p>
 <p>A recovery process should not re-run an arbitrary UPDATE expression against whatever data happens to be present. The original statement may have depended on a snapshot, a function result or a concurrent transaction. WAL instead gives PostgreSQL the storage-level information needed to reconstruct the recorded effects. Transaction records supply the associated outcomes.</p>
 <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-wal-records">WAL lesson</a> creates <code>pg_wal_example</code> with one record, available three. It also saves a log insertion position in a temporary marker table. Run its setup first, then execute:</p>
 <Sql code={update} label="Generate log records while leaving the transaction open" />
 <p><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> actually performs the update; the WAL option reports records, full-page images and bytes attributed to its work. This connection reads its own new value one, but no commit has occurred. Now predict whether rolling back will return the log position to its starting value.</p>
 <Sql code={rollback} label="Restore the logical result without erasing the WAL stream" />
 <p>The value returns to three and the insertion position remains ahead of the marker. Rollback did not erase the stream. This is necessary once later records from other transactions may already follow the update. The version and transaction outcome determine the logical result; the log remains a history of recovery work.</p>
 <p>The position difference is not an exact attribution of every byte to this one update. A server’s WAL stream also contains other transactions and internal work. Use statement-level WAL counters for that narrower observation, and treat even those counts as dependent on page state, indexes and checkpoint timing. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-internals.html">WAL internals</a> explain the stream and its records.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="positions"><h2>WAL insertion, writing and flushing</h2>
 <p>A <strong>log sequence number</strong>, or LSN, is a position in the WAL byte stream. PostgreSQL exposes several progress positions. Insertion means the record has entered the WAL machinery, potentially still in memory. Writing means bytes have been sent to the operating system. Flushing means PostgreSQL has completed the configured operation intended to make those bytes persistent. The last step depends on the operating system and storage honouring that request.</p>
 <Sql code={positions} label="Inspect different stages of local WAL progress" />
 <p>Our server experiment recorded the following values around one update and commit. The exact numbers are specific to that cluster; the relationship between them is what matters.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed WAL positions before and after commit"><table><thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th>Inserted</th><th>Written</th><th>Flushed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>After UPDATE, before COMMIT</td><td><code>0/18222D8</code></td><td><code>0/181D2A8</code></td><td><code>0/181D2A8</code></td></tr><tr><td>After durable local COMMIT</td><td><code>0/1822300</code></td><td><code>0/1822300</code></td><td><code>0/1822300</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The update had advanced insertion beyond the flush position. Commit added its outcome record and waited for the required WAL to become durable. In this quiet run, all three sampled positions then matched. On a busy system they need not remain equal after a commit, because other work can immediately insert more WAL. The guarantee concerns the acknowledged transaction’s required position, not permanent equality of the global counters. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-admin.html#FUNCTIONS-ADMIN-BACKUP">administration functions</a> define these observations.</p>
 <p>Two rules matter. A changed data page must not reach persistent storage before the WAL needed to recover its change. A durable local commit must not be acknowledged before its required WAL, including the commit record, is durable. These are different waiting points. They permit a data page to be written before commit, and they permit commit to be acknowledged before a data-page write.</p>
 <p>A single flush can cover records from several transactions. If their required positions are all within the flushed prefix, each has its local durability requirement satisfied. This is the basis of group commit. It shares a storage operation without weakening the promise made to each transaction. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html">WAL introduction</a> explains why flushing a sequential log can be cheaper than forcing every transaction’s scattered data pages.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="crash"><h2>Crash recovery with older data pages</h2>
 <p>We created two stock records, both initially three, and checkpointed that starting state. Transaction A changed record 1 to one and committed. The updated page was in memory. Reading the table file directly still produced exactly the same bytes as before A: its stored representation had not yet caught up with the committed change.</p>
 <p>Transaction B then changed record 2 to zero and stayed open. To make the test stronger, a separate connection committed unrelated marker work, flushing WAL beyond B’s update. We checked that the flush position covered B’s inserted record. Both updates now had durable recovery information, but only A had committed. The stock table file still matched the original bytes.</p>
 <p>We stopped this disposable cluster with an immediate shutdown, which omits a normal shutdown checkpoint, and restarted it. The server log reported automatic recovery and a redo starting position. The recovered query returned record 1 at one and record 2 at three.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Crash experiment distinguishes durable WAL from committed results"><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Starting value</th><th>Change before crash</th><th>WAL flushed?</th><th>Committed?</th><th>After restart</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>Record 1 demonstrates the need for redo: the old table-file bytes alone could not supply its committed result. Record 2 demonstrates why redo is not equivalent to making every logged version visible. Recovery can reconstruct physical changes belonging to an unfinished transaction without treating that transaction as committed. The original version remains the visible record, and abandoned versions become cleanup work.</p>
 <p>This experiment verifies PostgreSQL’s restart path with local files intact. It does not simulate a torn device write, prove that a storage controller survives power loss, or protect against losing the entire volume. Those claims require different failure tests and surviving resources. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-reliability.html">reliability documentation</a> follows the durability responsibility through the storage stack.</p>
 <p>For a safe application-level variation, use the browser lesson to change the uncommitted value to two and roll back. Predict the same separation: generated WAL can advance, but the final visible value is three. A crash test belongs in an isolated disposable server, never in the database serving the application being investigated.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="redo"><h2>Page LSNs and redo</h2>
 <p>After a real crash, stored pages need not all be equally old. A background write may have persisted one page while another remained dirty in memory. Replaying every change indiscriminately would risk applying an operation twice to a page that already contains it. Recovery needs progress information for each page.</p>
 <p>The page header includes a page LSN: the end position of the WAL record for its latest logged change. For ordinary page redo, PostgreSQL compares that progress with the record being replayed. If the page is already at or beyond the record’s position, the relevant change is already represented. If the page is behind, redo applies the necessary action and advances its progress. One record can refer to several pages whose stored progress differs.</p>
 <RedoProgress />
 <p>Suppose the stored page includes changes through P30, while recovery encounters records ending at P20 and P40. The earlier one does not need to be applied again to that page. The later one does. These labels stand for ordered positions in one WAL history, not transaction IDs. A high page LSN does not say every transaction whose bytes occur on the page committed.</p>
 <p>In our server run, the buffered stock page had LSN <code>0/18222D8</code> after A’s update, while commit advanced the stream to <code>0/1822300</code>. The commit outcome lives in the WAL and transaction machinery; it is not a requirement to rewrite every affected heap page with the commit record’s position. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-page-layout.html">page-header documentation</a> defines the field, and the <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/REL_18_STABLE/src/backend/access/transam/xlogutils.c">PostgreSQL 18 redo implementation</a> shows the comparison and full-page-image handling.</p>
 <p>This is also why recovery can safely encounter a mixture of pages written at different times. The WAL provides the ordered reconstruction history, and each page describes how far that page has already progressed. The transaction-status rules still decide which reconstructed versions are visible after recovery.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="checkpoint"><h2>Checkpoints and full-page images</h2>
 <p>Without another boundary, restart would have to consider an ever-growing WAL history. A checkpoint coordinates the required dirty-page writes and records a redo starting point. Once its writes and bookkeeping complete, recovery can rely on the earlier required page work being present and begin from that point. The redo position is associated with the checkpoint’s work; it is not simply the log’s end when checkpoint completion is reported.</p>
 <p>Transactions continue running during checkpointing. A page can be written for the checkpoint and then changed again. Finishing a checkpoint consequently does not mean every buffer is clean, and it does not commit transactions that are still open. It establishes a recoverable relationship between the required page writes and the retained log.</p>
 <p>A further problem remains: a page write itself may be interrupted. A PostgreSQL page is commonly 8 KiB, larger than the atomic unit guaranteed by a storage system. After failure it could contain a mixture of old and new portions. Applying a small change record to that damaged base would not necessarily reconstruct a valid page.</p>
 <p>With <code>full_page_writes</code> enabled, the first qualifying change to a page after a checkpoint causes a full-page image to be logged. Recovery can restore a coherent page image rather than trusting a potentially torn stored page, then apply subsequent records. The image’s representation can omit unused space and may be compressed, so one “full-page image” does not necessarily add exactly 8 KiB to the WAL. Our tiny stock fixture reported one image and 216 total WAL bytes for the measured update work.</p>
 <p>Without the image, a torn page cannot be repaired from the log, because a small change record has nothing coherent to apply to. Data checksums, on by default for clusters created by <code>initdb</code> in PostgreSQL 18, at least let recovery notice that a page fails verification and stop; without them the damage can pass unnoticed. Turn <code>full_page_writes</code> off only when the storage itself guarantees whole-page writes, such as ZFS, which the documentation names.</p>
 <p>That extra information has a workload cost. Frequent checkpoints repeatedly make pages eligible for first-change images and require page-write work. Longer intervals can retain more WAL and leave more work to replay after a crash. Checkpoint writes are spread over time to reduce bursts. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-configuration.html">checkpoint configuration discussion</a> explains this balance; choosing the shortest possible interval does not minimise all costs. If the server log warns that checkpoints are occurring too frequently, raising <code>max_wal_size</code> is the documented first response.</p>
 <p>The experiment’s single reported image is evidence that this logging path occurred, not a benchmark for an application’s WAL rate. A busy table with many changed pages, larger live contents and different checkpoint timing can generate very different volumes. Measure WAL bytes, full-page images, checkpoint activity and storage behaviour together before attributing a change to row size alone.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="reading-checkpoints"><h2>Reading checkpoints</h2>
 <p>Three things about checkpoints are worth watching: how often they happen, what started them, and how long their writing took. PostgreSQL reports all three in its server log, one pair of lines per checkpoint, because <code>log_checkpoints</code> is on by default. Here is one pair from a laptop running pgbench at 400 transactions a second, with <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> lowered to its 30-second minimum so that several cycles fit in two minutes.</p>
 <pre class="plan" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A checkpoint in the PostgreSQL server log"><code>{checkpointLog}</code></pre>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>What started it.</strong> The word after <code>checkpoint starting:</code> is the cause. <code>time</code> means <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> passed; <code>wal</code> means the log reached the limit set by <code>max_wal_size</code>. <code>immediate force wait</code> appears for a <code>CHECKPOINT</code> command, and <code>end-of-recovery</code> for the one that finishes crash recovery.</li>
  <li><strong>How much it wrote.</strong> 8,348 buffers, 51% of shared buffers.</li>
  <li><strong>How long the writing took.</strong> <code>write=27.013 s</code> is the design working: the writes are spread over 90% of the 30-second interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>). <code>sync=0.054 s</code> is the wait for the data files to be flushed at the end, and that is the number that grows when storage is slow.</li>
  <li><strong>How much log it covered.</strong> <code>distance=115037 kB</code> is the log written between this checkpoint’s start and the previous one’s: a log rate you get for free.</li>
  <li><strong>What happened to old log files.</strong> <code>7 recycled</code> means seven 16 MB segments were renamed for reuse. A run of checkpoints that remove and recycle nothing means something is holding the log, such as a failing archive or an abandoned slot.</li>
 </ul>
 <p><code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code>, which in PostgreSQL 17 and 18 holds the counters that used to sit in <code>pg_stat_bgwriter</code>, keeps the same story: <code>num_timed</code> for checkpoints started by the timer, <code>num_requested</code> for those requested by log size, a <code>CHECKPOINT</code> command or a backup, and <code>num_done</code> for those that finished.</p>
 <Sql code={checkpointer} label="Count timed and requested checkpoints" />
 <p>Under steady load most checkpoints should be timed. Many more requested than timed means log size is forcing them early, and PostgreSQL says so in the log whenever two such checkpoints start closer together than <code>checkpoint_warning</code>, 30 seconds by default:</p>
 <pre class="plan" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="PostgreSQL warning about frequent checkpoints"><code>{tooOften}</code></pre>
 <p>Who writes the pages matters as much as how many are written. The checkpointer writes pages for checkpoints, the background writer writes some early so queries find clean slots, and a session that needs a slot and finds a dirty page writes it itself, making its query wait. <code>pg_stat_io</code> counts each separately.</p>
 <Sql code={writers} label="Page writes by kind of process" />
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Page writes by kind of process in the local run"><table>
  <caption>Observed in the same run: two minutes, 30-second checkpoints, counters reset just before with <code>pg_stat_reset_shared('io')</code>.</caption>
  <thead><tr><th>backend_type</th><th>writes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td><code>checkpointer</code></td><td>34,165</td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>background writer</code></td><td>8,140</td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>client backend</code></td><td>19</td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>autovacuum worker</code></td><td>5</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>That is the healthy shape: the background processes do almost all the writing. The documentation names the other shape: many writes by client backends “could indicate a misconfiguration of shared buffers or of the checkpointer”. A partner signal is <code>maxwritten_clean</code> in <code>pg_stat_bgwriter</code>, the rounds in which the background writer stopped at its limit of 100 pages (<code>bgwriter_lru_maxpages</code>).</p>
 </section>
 <section id="max-wal-size"><h2>How max_wal_size sets checkpoint spacing</h2>
 <p>PostgreSQL wants each checkpoint to finish before the log reaches <code>max_wal_size</code>, and the log keeps growing while a checkpoint spreads its writes. So it starts the next checkpoint once the log written since the previous one began reaches the setting divided by one plus the share of the interval spent writing:</p>
 <p class="formula">trigger = max_wal_size ÷ (1 + checkpoint_completion_target)</p>
 <p>At the defaults, the trigger is about half the setting: 1 GB (1,024 MiB) divided by 1.9 is about 540 MiB. The server counts whole 16 MiB segments and rounds down, so a checkpoint starts after 512 to 528 MiB. The rule lives in the source (<code>CalculateCheckpointSegments</code> in <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/REL_18_STABLE/src/backend/access/transam/xlog.c"><code>xlog.c</code></a>), not the manual, so it may change between versions.</p>
 <p>The trigger is not the worst case. A crash just before a checkpoint finishes replays from where the previous checkpoint began: one trigger’s worth of log, plus everything written while the current checkpoint ran, close to <code>max_wal_size</code> in all.</p>
 <p>The figure draws this for an update-heavy load writing about 28 MiB of log a second. Watch the log a crash would replay climb until a checkpoint finishes, then drop. Move the slider to change <code>max_wal_size</code>, and choose a replay rate: 169 MiB a second, measured on the same laptop by crashing a copy under that load, or half that.</p>
 <StepFigure
  scene="checkpoint-sizing"
  number={1}
  title="How max_wal_size sets checkpoint spacing and the worst-case replay"
  alt="A chart over five minutes of how much log a crash would leave to replay, rising between checkpoints and dropping when each one finishes, against a dotted line at max_wal_size and a dashed line for what replays in 30 seconds."
  caption="Computed from PostgreSQL’s trigger rule for a steady 28 MiB of log a second, with every checkpoint finishing on schedule and no rounding to whole segments. In max_wal_size, 1 GB means 1,024 MiB. At this write rate, log size always starts a checkpoint before the 5-minute timer would."
 />
 <p>At the default 1 GB, a checkpoint starts every 19 seconds or so, and the worst case replays in about 6 seconds. The real server agreed: run for 45 seconds, the load started checkpoints at a <code>distance</code> of about 516 MiB, every 20 seconds or so, and logged the warning above. That leaves plenty of room under a 30-second recovery target, but it writes the same dirty pages over and over, and after each checkpoint the next change to every page logs a full-page image again.</p>
 <p>Raising <code>max_wal_size</code> trades that for longer replay. At 4 GB, a checkpoint starts after about 2.1 GiB, about every 77 seconds, and the worst case replays in about 24 seconds at 169 MiB a second, or about 48 at half that. <code>max_wal_size</code> is a soft limit: a checkpoint the disks can’t finish in time lets the log grow past it, so leave room below the ceiling. The method for every engine is in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/checkpoints-and-the-log/#choosing-interval">Checkpoints, torn pages and the log</a>.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="reading-restart"><h2>Reading a restart</h2>
 <p>After a crash, the server log is the record of recovery. To get a real example, a laptop server was stopped with <code>pg_ctl stop -m immediate</code>, which ends every process at once without a shutdown checkpoint, while pgbench ran flat out. The startup log follows, with dates and process IDs removed.</p>
 <pre class="plan" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="PostgreSQL crash recovery in the server log"><code>{restartLog}</code></pre>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>“last known up at 17:31:27”</strong> is when the control file was last updated, normally the last completed checkpoint. It is not the time of the crash, which was 17:32:07. A checkpoint had started at 17:32:06 but not finished, so it doesn’t count.</li>
  <li><strong>“redo starts at 0/47181CB8”</strong> is the start of that last completed checkpoint, and <strong>“redo done at 0/6A1D6808”</strong> the last record replayed. The positions are byte offsets, so recovery replayed 560 MiB, in 2.8 seconds on this laptop.</li>
  <li><strong>“invalid record length … expected at least 24, got 0”</strong> is how replay finds the end of the log: the next record is empty. Seen once, just before “redo done”, it is normal. If the next page belongs to a segment recycled from an earlier cycle, the same end is reported as “unexpected pageaddr”, because that page still carries its old address; a torn final write can show as an incorrect checksum instead. Tom Lane described “unexpected pageaddr” as <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/5031.1296689542%40sss.pgh.pa.us">one of the standard tests for detecting end of WAL</a>. Each is normal only at the true end of the log; replay ending well short of where the log stood is not.</li>
  <li><strong>“checkpoint starting: end-of-recovery”</strong> writes out everything replay changed, here nearly the whole pool, before the database opens.</li>
  <li><strong>“ready to accept connections”</strong> ends the outage, 3 seconds after the process started.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>A long replay is not silent: every 10 seconds (<code>log_startup_progress_interval</code>) PostgreSQL logs “redo in progress, elapsed time: …, current LSN: …”, and comparing that position with the end you expect shows how far it has got. The restart also resets the cumulative statistics, so a rate computed across it means nothing.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="flush"><h2>When a flush does not reach the disk</h2>
 <p>Every ordering rule above rests on one report: that the operating system has put the WAL on durable storage and says so. PostgreSQL asks with the method named by <code>wal_sync_method</code>, which is <code>fdatasync</code> on Linux, and it cannot check the answer. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-reliability.html">reliability documentation</a> advises avoiding disk controllers whose write cache has no battery, and turning off a drive’s write cache unless the drive guarantees it is written out before power is lost. The caches involved, and how to tell an honest drive from one that answers early, are explained in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#flush">Storage and durability</a>.</p>
 <p>To see what your own hardware does, run <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgtestfsync.html"><code>pg_test_fsync</code></a>, which times each flush method. A high rate means a fast, honest drive or a lying one, and only a <a href="/learn/databases/relational/checkpoints-and-the-log/#testing">pull-the-plug test</a> tells them apart. The settings under <a href="#policy">commit acknowledgement</a> below decide whether <code>COMMIT</code> waits for the flush at all.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="wal-flush-timing"><h2>Timing WAL flushes</h2>
 <p>A commit waits for its flush, so the flush time is the least a commit can take. PostgreSQL 18 reports log I/O in <code>pg_stat_io</code>, in the rows whose <code>object</code> is <code>wal</code>: <code>writes</code> and <code>fsyncs</code>, and with <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> on, which it is not by default, <code>write_time</code> and <code>fsync_time</code>. In PostgreSQL 17 these were columns of <code>pg_stat_wal</code>.</p>
 <p>Which column carries the flush depends on <code>wal_sync_method</code>. With <code>fdatasync</code>, the Linux default, every flush is a separate call, counted in <code>fsyncs</code> and timed in <code>fsync_time</code>. With an <code>open_datasync</code> or <code>open_sync</code> method, each write is itself synchronous, so the flush time sits inside <code>write_time</code> and <code>fsyncs</code> stays at zero. A laptop on macOS, where <code>open_datasync</code> is the default, showed exactly that: 47,076 log writes by sessions at about 0.12 ms each, and no fsyncs. That is not the cost of a durable flush; macOS acknowledges these writes before the drive has made them durable.</p>
 <p>Wait events point at the same place. A session waiting for its flush shows the IO wait <code>WalSync</code> (or <code>WalWrite</code> with an <code>open_*</code> method). Because of group commit, the other committing sessions queue behind it and show the lightweight lock <code>WALWrite</code>; it looks like a lock wait but is the same storage cost. Sessions in <code>SyncRep</code> are waiting for a synchronous standby instead. And <code>wal_buffers_full</code> in <code>pg_stat_wal</code> counts times the WAL buffers filled and had to be written early; a steady rise suggests a larger <code>wal_buffers</code>.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="policy"><h2>Commit acknowledgement and durability settings</h2>
 <p>With <code>synchronous_commit=off</code>, PostgreSQL replies once the commit record is in the WAL buffers, and the <strong>WAL writer</strong> flushes it soon after. A crash can then lose recently acknowledged transactions, and the changed promise is which recent successes are guaranteed to survive. The WAL writer wakes every 200 ms at the default <code>wal_writer_delay</code> and, when busy, prefers to write whole WAL pages, which is why the documentation sets the longest risk window at three times that delay: about 600 ms by default. Recovery replays what survived in order, so the database ends consistent but slightly behind what the application was told. This can be appropriate for work the application explicitly tolerates losing, but it is a different contract from the stock reservation’s durable acknowledgement. See <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-async-commit.html">asynchronous commit</a>.</p>
 <p>The setting can be changed for a single transaction, which suits writes the application can afford to lose, such as a page-view counter:</p>
 <Sql code={asyncCommit} label="Turn off synchronous commit for one transaction" />
 <p>Turning off <code>fsync</code> is different. It removes storage-ordering protections on which safe recovery depends, so the distinction matters when a performance experiment suggests “relaxing durability”: choosing to lose a recent consistent suffix of acknowledged work and risking damaged storage state are not interchangeable choices. With <code>fsync = off</code> PostgreSQL stops flushing anything, data files included, so an operating-system crash or power cut can leave the database corrupt beyond repair. It suits only a database you can rebuild from scratch.</p>
 <p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createtable.html#SQL-CREATETABLE-UNLOGGED"><strong>Unlogged tables</strong></a>, made with <code>CREATE UNLOGGED TABLE</code>, are a narrower trade: they skip the WAL, so writes are faster, and PostgreSQL empties them after any crash, committed rows included. Because replication ships the WAL, they are not copied to standbys either, so they come up empty after a failover.</p>
 <p>Synchronous replication extends the acknowledgement boundary to designated standbys. Depending on the setting, the writer can wait for remote writing, durable flushing or replay. Without configured synchronous standbys, <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> does not create a second copy. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT">setting definition</a> states the exact boundaries; the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">replication chapter</a> follows their consequences for read freshness and failover.</p>
 <p>Even a fully durable commit may have an uncertain caller outcome. The connection can fail after the log flush and before the reply arrives. Retrying a purchase blindly can duplicate its business effect. The application needs an operation identity and a way to discover whether that identity already committed. WAL protects the server’s history; it cannot guarantee delivery of the response.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="history"><h2>Point-in-time recovery</h2>
 <p>Crash recovery reconstructs the database’s valid recorded history, so it cannot undo a committed mistake or replace a lost volume. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/#pitr">Point-in-time recovery</a> is crash recovery started from an older copy, fed with archived WAL and stopped early. In PostgreSQL the pieces are a physical base backup, archived WAL and a recovery target, and the rest of this section is about what keeps that chain usable.</p>
 <p>The WAL sequence must cover the required interval and the appropriate history. A missing required segment breaks the reconstruction path; an archived file existing somewhere is insufficient unless the restore process can retrieve and use it. Timeline history matters after recovery creates a new branch. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">continuous-archiving documentation</a> develops these requirements.</p>
 <p>Retention is an operating obligation too. Failed archiving and stalled replication slots can hold WAL beyond the expected volume. More retained log is not automatically more recoverability if it fills the storage needed for the live server. Establish the consumer or recovery requirement keeping each history available, repair the dependency and test the restore procedure. Removing WAL files by hand bypasses those relationships.</p>
 <p>A useful drill verifies concrete promises: which acknowledged operation IDs survived, which uncommitted changes remained absent, how long replay took, and whether the restored application can serve correct requests. Our two-stock-record experiment is a small instance of that method. It makes the result derivable from the surviving WAL and transaction outcomes, instead of treating “recovery succeeded” as a sufficient explanation.</p>
 </section>
